      'In the late 1800s, scientists debated what actually protects the body: cells or substances in the blood. In 1883, Elie Metchnikoff observed cells that engulfed and digested microbes, which he called phagocytes ("eating cells"), and proposed that cells were the main defenders. This view became known as cell-mediated immunity.',
      'In 1890, Emil von Behring and Shibasaburo Kitasato showed that the serum (the fluid part of blood) from animals immunised against diphtheria could protect other animals when transferred. Something in the serum, which they called antitoxin, neutralised the diphtheria toxin. Other activities of serum were named precipitins and agglutinins. In the 1930s, the active substances were identified as the gamma-globulin (immunoglobulin) fraction of serum, the antibodies. Because this immunity was carried in body fluids (once called "humors"), it was called humoral immunity.',
      'It turned out that both sides were right. In the 1940s, Merrill Chase showed that immunity to tuberculosis could be transferred by white blood cells, not by serum. In the 1950s, the lymphocyte was found to be responsible for both kinds of immunity. Experiments in chickens showed that there are two kinds of lymphocyte: T lymphocytes, which mature in the thymus and are responsible for cellular immunity, and B lymphocytes, which produce antibodies. Later work showed how T cells develop in the thymus, how B cells secrete antibody and how dendritic cells activate T cells.',
