      'The theory now accepted is clonal selection, a refined version of Ehrlich’s idea. Each lymphocyte carries receptors for only one antigen, and its specificity is fixed before it ever meets that antigen. The body contains millions of different lymphocytes, each with a different receptor. When an antigen enters, it selects the lymphocytes whose receptors fit it, and these cells multiply to form a large clone of identical cells, all directed against that antigen.',
