      'It helps to distinguish infection from disease. Infection is the ability of a microbe to enter the body, multiply and provoke an immune response. Disease is when the infection causes signs and symptoms. Many infections never cause disease, because the immune system controls them.',
      'Immunity can be acquired in two ways, actively or passively, and each can happen naturally or artificially. In active immunity, the person’s own immune system responds to an antigen and makes antibodies and memory cells. It takes time to develop but lasts a long time. It is acquired naturally by having an infection, and artificially by vaccination.',
      'In passive immunity, a person receives ready-made antibodies from someone else. It works immediately but lasts only weeks to months, because the antibodies are gradually broken down and no memory cells are made. It is acquired naturally when a mother’s antibodies cross the placenta to the fetus or pass to the baby in breast milk, and artificially when antibodies are injected, for example antitoxin for tetanus or snake bites, or immunoglobulin for someone exposed to hepatitis or rabies.',
