      'Adaptive immunity develops after exposure to a particular antigen. It takes days to weeks to develop the first time, but it has three remarkable features. It is specific, recognising particular antigens precisely. It is diverse, able to recognise an enormous range of different antigens, at least a billion. And it has memory: after the first encounter, it responds much faster and more strongly the next time, which is why we usually get diseases such as measles only once, and why vaccines work.',
