      'The innate immune system recognises microbes not by their individual identity but by molecular patterns that are typical of whole groups of microbes. These are called pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs). They make good targets for three reasons: they are found on microbes but not on human cells, so the immune system does not attack itself; they are essential for the microbe’s survival, so the microbe cannot easily change them by mutation; and they are shared by many microbes, so a few receptors can detect a wide range of invaders. A PAMP acts like a fingerprint that marks a microbe as foreign.',
      'Examples include lipopolysaccharide (LPS), found in the outer wall of gram-negative bacteria; peptidoglycan, a component of bacterial cell walls; flagellin, the protein of bacterial flagella; double-stranded RNA, produced when many viruses replicate; and DNA containing unmethylated CpG sequences, which are common in bacterial and viral DNA but rare in ours.',
