      'PAMPs are detected by pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) on and inside innate immune cells. When a PRR binds its PAMP, it triggers a chain of signals inside the cell: adaptor proteins (such as MyD88 or TRIF) activate transcription factors (such as NF-κB or IRF), which switch on genes for cytokines. The cytokines cause inflammation, recruit more immune cells, produce fever and help start the adaptive immune response.',
