    1: [
      'Several properties determine how strongly a substance provokes an immune response. The first is foreignness: the immune system normally learns during development not to respond to the body’s own molecules, so substances that are more different from self tend to be more immunogenic.',
      'The second is size: very small molecules (below about 1000 daltons) are not immunogenic, those of moderate size may be, and large molecules (over about 6000 daltons) usually are. The third is chemical complexity: a molecule made of many different building blocks, such as a protein, is more immunogenic than one made of a single repeated unit. The fourth is degradability: antigen-presenting cells must be able to break the antigen down to present it to T cells, so substances that cannot be digested, such as polymers of D-amino acids, are poorly immunogenic.',
      'Other factors also matter. Particulate antigens, such as whole bacteria, are more immunogenic than soluble ones, and denatured proteins more than native ones, because they are more easily taken up by phagocytes. The route of entry matters: injection under the skin usually produces a stronger response than injection into a vein, which is stronger than swallowing. And genetics play a role, so some individuals respond strongly to an antigen and others poorly. Among types of molecule, proteins are the best immunogens; polysaccharides are usually good; nucleic acids and lipids are poor unless attached to carrier proteins.',
    ],
    2: [
      'An antibody or T-cell receptor does not recognise a whole antigen, but only a small part of it, called an epitope or antigenic determinant. The epitope fits into the antigen-binding site of the antibody, called the paratope, formed by its hypervariable regions. Most natural antigens, such as proteins and bacteria, have many epitopes (they are polyvalent), so they can bind many different antibodies; a hapten has only one (it is monovalent).',
      'Epitopes can be of two types. A conformational epitope is formed by amino acids that are brought close together by the folding of the protein, even if they are far apart in the sequence. Such epitopes are on the surface of the folded protein and are recognised by B cells and antibodies; they are lost if the protein unfolds. A linear epitope is a short continuous stretch of amino acids in the sequence. It may be on the surface or buried inside the protein, and linear epitopes are recognised mainly by T cells.',
      'B-cell and T-cell epitopes differ in several ways. B cells recognise epitopes on intact antigens, which may be proteins or polysaccharides, either conformational or linear, without needing MHC molecules. T cells recognise only short linear peptides, produced when proteins are broken down inside cells, and only when these peptides are presented on MHC molecules.',
    ],
    3: [
      'Antigens can be classified by where they come from. Exogenous antigens come from outside cells, such as bacteria and toxins that are inhaled, swallowed or injected. Antigen-presenting cells engulf them, break them down and present the fragments on MHC class II molecules to helper T cells. Endogenous antigens are made inside cells, for example viral proteins in an infected cell. They are presented on MHC class I molecules to cytotoxic T cells, which then kill the infected cell.',
      'Antigens can also be classified by their relation to the body. Xenoantigens come from other species; alloantigens come from other individuals of the same species, such as blood group antigens and the HLA antigens that cause transplant rejection; and autoantigens are the body’s own molecules that the immune system attacks in autoimmune disease. Some antigens are normally hidden from the immune system, such as proteins in the lens of the eye, and if released by injury they can provoke an autoimmune response. Heterophile antigens are similar molecules found in different species; for example, a protein of streptococcal bacteria resembles molecules in the heart and kidney, so antibodies against the bacteria can attack these organs, as in rheumatic fever.',
      'Tumour-specific antigens are found only on tumour cells, while tumour-associated antigens, such as α-fetoprotein and carcinoembryonic antigen, are present in large amounts on tumours but also in small amounts on normal cells; they are used as tumour markers. Bacterial exotoxins are strongly antigenic. Treated chemically, they become toxoids, which are no longer toxic but still provoke antibodies (antitoxins); toxoids are used as vaccines against tetanus and diphtheria.',
      'Antigens also differ in whether they need T-cell help. T-dependent antigens, usually proteins with many different epitopes, require helper T cells to stimulate B cells. They produce several classes of antibody (IgG, IgA and IgM), cell-mediated immunity and memory. T-independent antigens, usually polysaccharides with many identical repeating epitopes, can stimulate B cells directly, but they produce mainly IgM and little or no memory, which is why polysaccharide vaccines work poorly in young children unless conjugated to a protein.',
    ],
    4: [
      'An adjuvant is a substance that boosts or changes the immune response to an antigen when given with it, without being an antigen itself. Adjuvants are added to many vaccines. They work in several ways: they change the physical form of the antigen, often making it particulate, so it is more easily taken up by phagocytes; they help macrophages process and present it; they stimulate lymphocytes non-specifically; and they hold the antigen at the injection site, releasing it slowly so the immune system is stimulated for longer.',
      'Examples include aluminium salts (alum), the most widely used adjuvant in human vaccines; BCG (the tuberculosis vaccine bacteria); poly I:C, a synthetic double-stranded RNA that mimics viral infection; and Freund’s adjuvant, used in animal research, in which the antigen is mixed with mineral oil; the complete form also contains killed mycobacteria, which stimulate macrophages strongly.',
      'Mitogens are substances that make a large proportion of T or B cells divide, regardless of their antigen specificity. Concanavalin A and phytohaemagglutinin stimulate T cells; pokeweed mitogen stimulates both T and B cells; and LPS stimulates B cells. Mitogens are used in the laboratory to test whether lymphocytes can respond. Superantigens are the most powerful T-cell mitogens, activating huge numbers of T cells at once, which causes toxic shock.',
    ],
